Fix: valid tool calls dropped instead of salvaged at parse time.

Confirmed live in ~/.local/state/lumo-tamer/openroam-server.log across two
distinct failure shapes, both ending with a well-formed tool call thrown away:

1. Trailing garbage glued onto an otherwise-complete object. Whenever Lumo
   emits two tool calls back-to-back in one turn, the FIRST one repeatedly
   fails with "Invalid tool call (malformed JSON)" while later calls in the
   same burst succeed:

     Invalid tool call (malformed JSON): {"name": "user:org-roam_search_nodes",
       "arguments": {"query": "openroam"}} ```

   The logged content is a complete, valid JSON object immediately followed
   by a stray "```" with nothing else after it -- consistent with a chunk
   boundary splitting the three backtick characters of a fence marker (e.g.
   2 land at the tail of one chunk, 1 at the head of the next) so neither
   processCodeFenceState()'s in-chunk search nor its buffer-suffix check
   recognizes the fence, and the remnant ends up glued onto the end of an
   otherwise-complete buffered object. JSON.parse() then throws on the
   trailing garbage and the whole call is discarded as inert text.

2. A fence that never closes at all before the stream ends. Also confirmed
   live: Lumo emitted ```json\n{"name": "user:org-roam_create_node", ...}
   with no closing ``` before the stream terminated. finalize()'s end-of-
   stream salvage already retries JSON.parse for a dangling in_raw_json
   buffer, but does nothing for a dangling in_code_fence buffer -- it just
   dumps the buffer as text, even when the JSON inside it is perfectly
   valid. This is the one that mattered most in practice: it's the step
   that actually creates the note, so this alone was enough to make the
   capture agent produce nothing on every run that reached it.

Fix, in two parts:
- When JSON.parse() fails, scan for the first complete, balanced {...}
  object at the start of the content and retry on just that substring
  before giving up. This discards trailing noise (a stray fence remnant,
  or the start of the next block) instead of failing the whole parse --
  the next block still gets its own independent detection pass, as
  already observed in the log for the calls that follow a corrupted one.
- finalize()'s end-of-stream salvage now retries a dangling in_code_fence
  buffer the same way it already does for in_raw_json, so a fence that
  never closes no longer forfeits an otherwise-valid tool call.

--- a/src/api/tools/streaming-tool-detector.ts
+++ b/src/api/tools/streaming-tool-detector.ts
@@ -249,6 +249,50 @@
   }

   /**
+   * Scan for the first complete, balanced {...} object at the start of
+   * content, ignoring any trailing text after it. Recovers tool calls
+   * where a chunk-boundary race glues a stray fence marker (or the start
+   * of the next block) onto an otherwise-complete JSON object - see
+   * regression test for the exact failure mode this guards against.
+   */
+  private extractLeadingJsonObject(content: string): string | null {
+    const start = content.indexOf('{');
+    if (start === -1) return null;
+
+    let depth = 0;
+    let inString = false;
+    let escaped = false;
+
+    for (let i = start; i < content.length; i++) {
+      const char = content[i];
+
+      if (inString) {
+        if (escaped) {
+          escaped = false;
+        } else if (char === '\\') {
+          escaped = true;
+        } else if (char === '"') {
+          inString = false;
+        }
+        continue;
+      }
+
+      if (char === '"') {
+        inString = true;
+      } else if (char === '{') {
+        depth++;
+      } else if (char === '}') {
+        depth--;
+        if (depth === 0) {
+          return content.slice(start, i + 1);
+        }
+      }
+    }
+
+    return null;
+  }
+
